# The oracle references, pinned.
#
# One line per reference, tab-separated: <name> <image> <version> <what it
# answers>.
#
# **Every line names the same image.** These are not two tools each answering
# about its own format; they are two OBJ readers asked the same question, and
# the whole value is in where they disagree. In two images with two base
# states, a disagreement would first have to be shown not to be the images'
# fault. One image makes "the reference set" one fact.
#
# The image is built here, so the tag is not a digest of the finished image:
# two builds of one Containerfile are not two copies of one image. What is
# pinned instead is everything the build reads - the base by digest, the
# packages by full apt version, resolved against a dated snapshot of the
# Debian archive so the pins do not expire - and the run-time version check
# is the real guarantee. oracle_env.check_pin() therefore runs in both modes.
# There is no stock image named here, so relaxing the check in host mode would
# leave nothing checking anything.
#
# The tag is Blender's version, then f3d's: 4.3.2-3.1.0. The version field
# names what decides an answer, which for f3d is not f3d. f3d is how VTK's
# vtkOBJReader is reached, and a newer VTK behind the same f3d package is a
# different reader, so the field names both.
#
# Raising a pin is deliberate. A new reference version is new answers, and a
# new answer that disagrees is a finding to triage rather than a regression
# to fix.
#
# Building it:
#   make oracle-build

blender	localhost/ghoti-model-oracle-readers:4.3.2-3.1.0	Blender 4.3.2	Blender's OBJ importer, and the only reader of the MTL statements VTK declines
f3d	localhost/ghoti-model-oracle-readers:4.3.2-3.1.0	F3D 3.1.0, VTK 9.3.0.	vtkOBJReader, reached through f3d because VTK ships no OBJ binary of its own
